@meta
id: 21-14
title: Fix the macro, not the file
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-edit, macro-count
par: 42
minutes: 8
vscode: no equivalent; you would re-do the whole multi-cursor dance
open: orders.fixture.ts
vimrc: let @a="^i{ id: '\<Esc>f,s', customerId: '\<Esc>f,s', total: \<Esc>f,s, curency: '\<Esc>A' },\<Esc>j"

@brief
A colleague left the macro for this fixture in register `a`. Look at it
with `:reg a`. It turns a row into a record:

    ord_101,cus_7,1200,GBP    →    { id: 'ord_101', customerId: 'cus_7', total: 1200, currency: 'GBP' },

It has a typo. Run it once and you will see `curency`. It is a long
macro and one letter is wrong, so do not re-record it. A macro is
text in a register: put it in the buffer, fix the letter as you would
fix any other text, yank it back into the register, and then run it
on all sixteen rows.

@keys
| `"ap` | put register `a` into the file as text |
| `0"ay$` | yank the whole line, without its newline, back into `a` |
| `:reg a` | show what `a` contains |
| `dd` | remove the scratch line afterwards |

@hints
Open a blank line at the bottom with `o`, `Esc`, then `"ap`. The macro
appears as text; each `^[` is an `Esc`, one character wide.
--
Find `curency` and add the missing `r`. Then `0"ay$` yanks the corrected
line back into `a`. Not `"ayy`: that takes the newline too, and a
newline inside a macro is an extra `j`.
--
`Go<Esc>"ap`, `$Fu` `ar` `Esc`, `0"ay$`, `dd`, `ggj`, `16@a`.

@solution
    G o <Esc> "ap   $Fu ar <Esc>   0"ay$   dd   ggj   16@a   :wq<Enter>

Thirty keystrokes, against about seventy to record it again.

A macro is a register and a register is text. That is the whole trick.
`"ap` puts the text where you can see it; the `Esc` keys inside it are
real characters, shown as `^[`, and they survive being pasted and
yanked. Fix the text with the same keys you use on any line. `0"ay$`
puts it back.

The reason for `y$` rather than `yy` is precise. `yy` is linewise and
includes the line break, and when a macro contains a line break it
runs it, which moves the cursor down a line. That is one extra `j` on
every replay, and every second row is skipped. `0"ay$` yanks from
column one to the end and no further. Then `dd` removes the scratch
line; that goes into the unnamed register and leaves `a` alone.

You will do this more often than you expect. The macro that nearly
works is the common case, and re-recording a long one from scratch is
how a five second fix becomes a two minute one.

@start orders.fixture.ts
export const orders = [
  ord_101,cus_7,1200,GBP
  ord_102,cus_7,450,GBP
  ord_103,cus_12,9900,EUR
  ord_104,cus_3,1575,GBP
  ord_105,cus_21,80,USD
  ord_106,cus_12,2200,EUR
  ord_107,cus_8,640,GBP
  ord_108,cus_8,640,GBP
  ord_109,cus_30,12000,USD
  ord_110,cus_3,315,GBP
  ord_111,cus_17,1999,EUR
  ord_112,cus_21,4250,USD
  ord_113,cus_9,75,GBP
  ord_114,cus_12,3600,EUR
  ord_115,cus_4,880,GBP
  ord_116,cus_30,150,USD
];

